# .dockerignore — controls what gets sent to the Docker build context
# Works just like .gitignore. Excluded files are never sent to the daemon.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS:
#   - Keeps sensitive files (like .env) out of your image
#   - Speeds up builds (smaller context = faster upload to Docker daemon)
#   - Avoids accidentally caching secrets in image layers
#   - Prevents huge node_modules from bloating your build context

# Node.js dependencies — always reinstall with npm install inside the image
node_modules

# Git history — not needed inside a container
.git

# All markdown files — docs are for humans, not containers
*.md

# Environment files — NEVER bake secrets into images!
# Pass secrets at runtime using: docker run -e KEY=value or --env-file
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Common extras worth adding in real projects:
# .DS_Store        ← macOS metadata
# *.log            ← log files
# dist/            ← build artifacts (rebuild inside container)
# coverage/        ← test coverage reports
# .pytest_cache/   ← Python test cache
# __pycache__/     ← Python bytecode